 If the octagon has $n$ angles greater than $90^{\circ}$, then the other $8-n$ angles must be less than $90^{\circ}$, and hence acute.  The sum of the interior angles of an $n$-gon is $180(n-2)$ degrees.  Therefore, the sum of the remaining $8-n$ acute angles is less than $90(8-n)$.  Since the remaining angles are acute, the sum of their degrees is less than $720-90n$.  Since the sum of the $n$ obtuse angles is greater than $90n$, we have  \((180n-720)<(8-n)\cdot90 \Longrightarrow n<4\), \alomujim1. Therefore, the greatest number of interior right angles a convex octagon can have is $\boxed{3}$.
